From: Keith Dilsworth <kdd@.zko.dec.com>
Subject: Re: Complete system failure following reboot
Date: 2000/07/27
Message-ID: <6TuAOXd6p6VgLCTmVQK=6FmGc=IS@4ax.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 651406934
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
References: <Pine.SOL.3.96.1000726174307.28588A-100000@thale>
Organization: Digital Equipment Corporation, Palo Alto, CA, USA
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.unix.tru64

You could boot into single user mode and take a look around.

>>>  boot -fl s

After/if it boots you can either run "bcheckrc" and try to mount
everything or "mount -u /" to get write access to root disk.

You will find all your AdvFS domains in /etc/fdmns  They are
directories with a symbolic link to the file that is the device
holding the domain.  i.e. /etc/fdmns/root_domain/dsk0a is a symbolic
link to /dev/disk/dsk0a    Make sure all the AdvFS domain links look
good.

The next thing is to check the devices them selves.  "disklabel dsk0"
should read the disklabel of dsk0.   These two should give you a
starting point.

keith



