> I have a question about what this command: "uname -n" is supposed to return.
> On Solaris, it returns my hostname, but on Tru64, it returns
> host.domain.com. I have a few scripts that run fine on other Unix platforms
> but failed on Tru64. Could someone explain to me why it is like this? Is it
> something I did wrong while setting up the system? I am running Tru64 5.0.
>
> I want to know if this is when Compaq decided to change "uname -n" to return
> the entire hostname and domain name? Which verision begins this unique
> behavior so that I can adjust my script to check on the version accordingly.


Tru64 Unix uname -n is documented as:

 -n  Displays the name of the node (this *may* be a name that the system
is
      known by to a communications network).

Similar wording is used in other uname man pages that I've seen on the
web for AIX, SOLARIS, and other vendors.  In fact, the Solaris man page
says "....the nodename *is* the name by which the system is known to a
communications network..." - see
http://www.cs.rit.edu/~hpb/Man/_Man_Solaris_2.6_html/html1/uname.1.html. 
In any case, Tru64 Unix uname -n displays the name as set in
/etc/rc.config HOSTNAME.  This file is typically generated as system
installation time and HOSTNAME is chosen by the person performing the
installation.

Tony V.
Kernel Group



