On Wed, 12 Jul 2000 21:13:38 +0200, "Mickael GASTINEAU" <amgastineau@voonoo.net>
wrote:

>I have some trouble to print long double data on Tru64 Unix5.0A with the
>system call printf(3).
>After the 16th digit, it always print 0 for the digits.
>But long double has about 33 significant digits on Tru64Unix5.0A.
>[...]

You need a better standard C library.

The simplest approach to printing a floating point number is to scale the number
by powers of 10 into the range of the largest available integer type, then to
convert that to a string, giving a decimal significand.

If you're only getting 16 decimal digits, it's probably because your library is
using a 64-bit integer for the intermediate representation. Other libraries
(e.g. glibc) use arbitrary precison integer arithmetic to give effectively
unlimited precision.

-- Mat.




