From: "Eckhardt Wallmeroth" <ewallmeroth@de.lhsgroup.com>
Subject: Re: virtual memory size
Date: 2000/07/20
Message-ID: <8l64ll$3q02u$1@ID-41025.news.cis.dfn.de>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 648498418
References: <3974A56A.2C51AAFB@unh.edu>
X-Priority: 3
X-MimeOLE: Produced By Microsoft MimeOLE V5.00.2314.1300
X-Trace: fu-berlin.de 964072950 3997790 208.140.124.146 (16 [41025])
X-MSMail-Priority: Normal
Newsgroups: comp.unix.tru64

the VSZ of the kernel doesn't show you the virtual memory limits set on your
machine. Do a 'ulimit -a' (sh,ksh) or 'limit' (csh) command and look for
data/vmemory resp. datasize/addressspace to see your current limits.

To get your maximum and default values do '/sbin/sysconfig -q proc' and look
for the [max-]per-proc-* values.
per-proc-* is what you get after login; max-per-proc-* is where you can go
to with the 'ulimit -.../unlimit' command.

If the max-per-proc-* values are not sufficient, you have to change their
values in /etc/sysconfigtab. Either edit the file (be careful, its a bit
sensitive about the syntax) or use the 'sysconfigdb' command (the official
way).

Eckhardt





